# Career Log Capture

When the user gives a trigger phrase (e.g. "log this ticket", "ticket done, log it",
"career log this") after completing work on a ticket/issue, do the following:

## 1. Gather context
Look at the current conversation and recent file changes to reconstruct what the
ticket was about: the problem, the files/systems touched, and any PR description
or commit messages available. If something important is unclear or missing, ask
the user directly rather than guessing — especially:
- Who else was involved (teammates coordinated with, unblocked, or mentored)
- Any decision the user made about scope, priority, or approach that others
  deferred to
- Any moment the user explained a technical decision to a non-technical
  stakeholder (PM, support, client)
- The measurable or observable outcome (bug rate, downtime avoided, feature
  shipped, time saved, teammate now able to work independently on X)

## 2. Filter for signal
Not every ticket has leadership content. If, after gathering context, the ticket
was purely solo technical work with no coordination, mentoring, scoping decision,
or stakeholder communication — say so plainly and ask the user if they still want
it logged (e.g. for a pure technical-depth resume line) or skipped. Don't force
management framing onto tickets that don't have it.

## 3. Write the entry
Append a new entry to `career-log.md` in the project root (create the file with a
top-level `# Career Log` heading if it doesn't exist yet). Use this structure:

```markdown
## [YYYY-MM-DD] <Short ticket/project title>

**Scope owned:** <what the user was responsible for>

**Coordination / mentoring:** <who was involved, what was unblocked or taught —
or "None" if solo>

**Decision made:** <any scoping, prioritization, or technical-direction call the
user made — or "None">

**Stakeholder communication:** <any explanation of technical decisions to a
non-technical party — or "None">

**Impact:** <concrete, quantified outcome where possible>

**Resume-ready line:** <one polished sentence, outcome-first, written the way it
would appear on a resume or LinkedIn — only include this if step 2 found real
leadership signal>
```

## 4. Confirm
After appending, tell the user in one or two lines what was logged — don't repeat
the whole entry back, they can open the file. If the entry had no real leadership
signal, say that plainly instead of manufacturing one.

## Notes
- Never invent details (people, numbers, outcomes) the user hasn't confirmed —
  ask instead of guessing.
- Keep entries factual and specific. Vague entries ("helped the team") are
  useless six months later when writing a resume bullet from this log.
